  inside an HTTP request or bolting a queue on afterward. Complexity: trivial.

  [1mB. Task event table, then fan-out.[0m Every mutation writes a [38;5;153mtask_event[39m row ([38;5;153mtask_id[39m, [38;5;153mactor_id[39m, [38;5;153mkind[39m, [38;5;153mpayload[39m,
  [38;5;153mcreated_at[39m) in the same transaction as the edit. A separate step reads events and writes per-user notification rows.
  This is the event seam we agreed spec 1 should leave: email and push become additional consumers of the same stream
  rather than edits to the notification code, and you get a task activity history for free, which a task app wants
  anyway. The cost is one extra table and a fan-out step to own. Complexity: moderate.

  [1mC. Derive the inbox at read time.[0m Store no notification rows at all. The inbox is a query joining [38;5;153mtask_event[39m against
  your subscriptions, filtered by a per-user [38;5;153mlast_read_at[39m watermark. Nothing to fan out, nothing to keep consistent, and
  the unread count is a [38;5;153mCOUNT(*)[39m. The cost lands on per-notification state: marking one item read, or dismissing a
  single notification while keeping later ones unread, doesn't fit a watermark, and retrofitting that means adopting B
  anyway. Complexity: trivial now, moderate later.

  [1mI recommend B, with A's operational simplicity kept.[0m Write the event row in the transaction, then run the fan-out
  in-process immediately after commit — no queue, no worker process, no broker. That gets B's data model, which is the
  part that's expensive to change, while the infrastructure stays as boring as A's. When email shows up, the fan-out
  moves behind a real worker and nothing above it changes.

  C tempts me for how little it is, but "mark this one read" is table stakes for an inbox, and that's exactly the thing
  a watermark can't express.
